     The ssiiggsseett() function modifies signal dispositions.  The _s_i_g argument
     specifies the signal, which may be any signal except SIGKILL and SIGSTOP.
     The _d_i_s_p argument specifies the signal's disposition, which may be
     SIG_DFL, SIG_IGN, or the address of a signal handler.  If ssiiggsseett() is
     used, and _d_i_s_p is the address of a signal handler, the system adds _s_i_g to
     the signal mask of the calling process before executing the signal han‐
     dler; when the signal handler returns, the system restores the signal
     mask of the calling process to its state prior to the delivery of the
     signal.  In addition, if ssiiggsseett() is used, and _d_i_s_p is equal to SIG_HOLD,
     _s_i_g is added to the signal mask of the calling process and _s_i_g _'_s dispo‐
     sition remains unchanged.  If ssiiggsseett() is used, and _d_i_s_p is not equal to
     SIG_HOLD, _s_i_g is removed from the signal mask of the calling process.

     The ssiigghhoolldd() function adds _s_i_g to the signal mask of the calling
     process.

     The ssiiggrreellssee() function removes _s_i_g from the signal mask of the calling
     process.

     The ssiiggiiggnnoorree() function sets the disposition of _s_i_g to SIG_IGN.

     The xxssii__ssiiggppaauussee() function removes _s_i_g from the signal mask of the call‐
     ing process and suspend the calling process until a signal is received.
     The xxssii__ssiiggppaauussee() function restores the signal mask of the process to
     its original state before returning.

     The ssiiggppaauussee() function assigns _s_i_g_m_a_s_k to the set of masked signals and
     then waits for a signal to arrive; on return the set of masked signals is
